/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Happiness and relationships */
WB.L("happiness-science", "emotions", "beginner", "What actually makes people happy",
  "Money, success, relationships, genes? Decades of research give some surprising and reassuring answers.",
  [
    ["Measuring happiness", "Happiness research separates overall life satisfaction from daily mood, and measures both.",
      "Psychologists usually split happiness, or subjective wellbeing, into two parts: how satisfied you are with your life as a whole, and how you feel from moment to moment. Life satisfaction is often measured with the Cantril ladder, which asks people to rate their life from 0, the worst possible, to 10, the best possible. Daily emotions are tracked with diaries and phone apps that ping people at random times.\n\nThe World Happiness Report, published since 2012 using Gallup World Poll data, ranks countries each year. Finland has topped it for many years running, Nordic neighbours sit close behind, and Australia regularly places in the top 20."],
    ["Relationships come first", "The Harvard study found good relationships are the clearest predictor of long, healthy, happy lives.",
      "The Harvard Study of Adult Development began in 1938, following Harvard students alongside young men from poor Boston neighbourhoods, and was later extended to their children. It's one of the longest-running studies of adult life ever conducted. Its fourth director, psychiatrist Robert Waldinger, summarised the findings: good relationships are the clearest predictor of long, healthy, happy lives, more than wealth, fame or social class.\n\nPeople most satisfied in their relationships at 50 were the healthiest at 80, as if warm connections buffered the knocks of ageing. Loneliness, by contrast, is linked to poorer health and shorter lives. Quality mattered more than the number of relationships."],
    ["Money and genes", "Money helps most when it relieves hardship, and genes shape wellbeing without fixing it.",
      "Money affects happiness, especially when it lifts people out of poverty and financial stress. A well-known 2010 study by Daniel Kahneman and Angus Deaton suggested daily emotional wellbeing stopped rising above about US$75,000 a year. In 2023, Kahneman and Matthew Killingsworth found happiness keeps rising with income for most people, but levels off for the least happy fifth or so. Research by Elizabeth Dunn and Michael Norton found spending on others lifts mood more than spending on yourself.\n\nTwin studies suggest roughly a third to a half of differences in wellbeing is linked to genes. A popular claim that 50% is genetic, 10% circumstances and 40% deliberate has been criticised as too neat. Genes set tendencies, not destiny."],
    ["Habits that help", "Exercise, sleep, kindness, time outdoors and absorbing 'flow' activities reliably lift wellbeing.",
      "Regular physical activity is one of the most reliable mood boosters, and even a brisk walk helps. After a poor night's sleep, people rate their mood and patience noticeably lower. Acts of kindness increase happiness in the giver, a finding repeated in studies across many countries. Time outdoors in green spaces and near water is linked to better mood and lower stress.\n\nPsychologist Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi found people are often happiest in 'flow', fully absorbed in an activity that stretches their skills, like playing music, surfing, gardening or engaging work. Flow tends to happen when the challenge sits just a little beyond what feels comfortable."],
    ["Don't chase it directly", "Chasing happiness can backfire; it tends to arrive as a by-product of living well, and persistent low mood deserves professional help.",
      "Economists reported that life satisfaction tends to follow a gentle U-shape, dipping in the mid-40s to early 50s and rising again later, though the dip's size is contested. The stereotype of a gloomy old age isn't supported.\n\nStudies by Iris Mauss found people who place great value on happiness, and constantly check whether they're happy enough, can end up less happy. Setting a high bar turns ordinary moments into disappointments. Happiness tends to arrive as a by-product of connecting with people, doing meaningful things and getting absorbed. Accepting negative emotions as normal is linked to better wellbeing.\n\nProtect time for people, move most days, guard your sleep, and spend on shared experiences or other people. If low mood persists for weeks, raise it with a GP; depression is common and treatable."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What are the two parts psychologists measure in happiness?", ["Income level and job status","Physical health and fitness","Family size and friendships","Life satisfaction and daily mood"], 3, "Wellbeing covers how you rate your life overall and how you feel day to day."],
    [1, "What did the Harvard study find best predicts a long, healthy, happy life?", ["Good relationships","High income","Fame and recognition","Family background"], 0, "Warm, satisfying relationships mattered more than wealth, fame or social class."],
    [2, "Which kind of spending tends to lift mood most?", ["Spending on expensive possessions","Saving every cent you earn","Spending on others and on experiences","Buying the latest gadgets"], 2, "Research found spending on others lifts mood more than spending on yourself."],
    [3, "What is 'flow'?", ["Resting with no demands on your attention","Being fully absorbed in an activity that stretches your skills","Feeling a rush of excitement from winning","Doing an easy task on autopilot"], 1, "Flow happens when a challenge sits just beyond your comfort zone, like playing music or surfing."],
    [4, "What can happen when people constantly check whether they're happy enough?", ["They reach their goals faster","They become more sociable","They become more resilient","They can end up less happy"], 3, "A high bar turns ordinary moments into disappointments, so happiness is better found as a by-product."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The best predictor of a long, happy life isn't wealth or fame. It's simpler."});

WB.L("hedonic-adaptation", "emotions", "intermediate", "Why new things stop making us happy",
  "The new car, the pay rise, the bigger house. The thrill fades fast. Understanding the 'hedonic treadmill' can save you money and disappointment.",
  [
    ["The hedonic treadmill", "People adapt to good and bad changes, returning towards their usual level of happiness.",
      "In 1971, Brickman and Campbell described the 'hedonic treadmill': as life improves, expectations rise with it, so we end up feeling much as before. A pay rise thrills for a few months, then becomes normal, and we want the next one.\n\nA 1978 study compared 22 Illinois lottery winners with similar people who hadn't won. The winners weren't significantly happier, and they enjoyed everyday pleasures, like chatting with a friend, less. People recently paralysed were less happy, but less unhappy than most would predict. The study was small, and larger recent studies find modest, lasting gains in life satisfaction from big wins. Adaptation also varies, and some changes, like disability or long-term unemployment, can leave a lasting mark."],
    ["Why we adapt", "Our brains notice change, so constant good things fade into the background, and we judge ourselves against others.",
      "Our nervous systems notice change more than steady states. A strong smell fades within minutes, and so do a humming fridge, a new sofa and a once-stunning view. Anything constant stops carrying new information. Evolution may explain why: permanent satisfaction wouldn't have pushed our ancestors to seek food, safety or better shelter. The same system helps people recover from setbacks.\n\nAdaptation has a partner, comparison. Economist Richard Easterlin noted in 1974 that richer people within a country were happier, yet average happiness in the United States hadn't risen much as the nation grew richer. This Easterlin paradox is still debated. Other research finds people report lower wellbeing when neighbours earn more. Social media makes the comparison group enormous."],
    ["We mispredict our feelings", "People overestimate how long and how strongly events will affect their happiness.",
      "Psychologists Daniel Gilbert and Timothy Wilson study 'affective forecasting': how well we predict our future feelings. We're surprisingly poor at it. People overestimate how happy a new job, house or partner will make them, and how devastated they'd be by a breakup, a rejection or a lost election. This mistake is called the impact bias.\n\nOne cause is focalism: we zoom in on the change and forget the ordinary things that will still fill our days, like commuting, cooking and chatting. Another is that we underestimate our 'psychological immune system', the natural ways we make sense of setbacks and cope with them. Expect both good and bad events to fade sooner than you think."],
    ["Slowing adaptation", "Variety, savouring, gratitude and spacing out pleasures help good things keep giving.",
      "Several habits can slow adaptation. Add variety: change how, where or with whom you enjoy something. Savour pleasant moments by pausing to notice them, and share them with others, which tends to amplify them. Practise gratitude by naming what you already have. Imagine losing something, a Stoic technique often called negative visualisation.\n\nSpacing pleasures out helps too. A 2009 study led by Leif Nelson found people enjoyed TV shows more when ad breaks interrupted them, because the interruptions reset adaptation. Ending a good thing slightly early can leave you wanting more rather than bored."],
    ["Choosing with adaptation", "Weigh daily, ongoing factors more heavily than one-off features, because we adapt least to experiences, relationships and ongoing stresses.",
      "Some things resist adaptation. Experiences keep giving through anticipation, memories and the stories we tell, and they're harder to compare with other people's. Close relationships renew through shared growth. Some ongoing stresses persist too. Economists Alois Stutzer and Bruno Frey found people with long commutes reported lower life satisfaction, and higher pay or cheaper housing didn't seem to fully make up for it. Persistent noise and chronic pain keep demanding attention.\n\nSo weigh daily factors above one-off features. A bigger kitchen soon feels normal, but a 90-minute commute is felt every working day. Ask how you'll feel in six months, not six days, and picture an ordinary Tuesday in the new situation."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the 1978 lottery study suggest about big windfalls?", ["Winners returned close to their usual happiness and enjoyed everyday pleasures less","Winners stayed far happier than non-winners for decades","Winners became noticeably less happy than people paralysed in accidents","Winners were happier but only while spending the money"], 0, "Adaptation pulls us back towards our usual level of happiness, even after a huge change."],
    [1, "Why do good things fade into the background?", ["Pleasant experiences gradually damage the brain's reward system","Our brains notice change, and constant things stop carrying new information","People deliberately lower their expectations to avoid disappointment","We forget good events faster than bad ones"], 1, "Nervous systems respond most to change, so anything constant is quickly tuned out."],
    [2, "What is the impact bias?", ["Judging events by how they ended rather than how they felt","Giving more weight to bad events than to good ones","Overestimating how long and how strongly events will affect our feelings","Underestimating how much other people notice our mistakes"], 2, "We focus on the change and forget the ordinary life that carries on around it."],
    [3, "Which approach helps keep a pleasure feeling fresh?", ["Upgrading to a bigger version of it straight away","Enjoying it as often as possible to build the habit","Adding variety and spacing it out, even interrupting it","Avoiding thinking about it so you don't get used to it"], 2, "Variety and breaks reset adaptation, which is why ad breaks improved enjoyment of TV."],
    [4, "What does adaptation research suggest when choosing between two homes?", ["Give extra weight to the daily commute, since people adapt to it poorly","Choose the bigger kitchen, since that pleasure lasts for years","Trust the excitement of the first inspection above everything else","Ignore the commute, since people soon get used to it"], 0, "One-off features soon feel normal, but ongoing daily stresses keep being felt."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A new car thrills for weeks, then vanishes into the background. Why?"});

WB.L("gratitude", "emotions", "beginner", "Gratitude",
  "One of the simplest, most researched ways to feel better. And it takes about five minutes.",
  [
    ["More than good manners", "Gratitude is noticing the good in your life and recognising its sources lie partly outside yourself, and weekly practice lifted mood in a key study.",
      "Gratitude means affirming that there is goodness in your life and recognising that its sources lie partly outside yourself, in other people or in luck. Psychologist Robert Emmons describes it in these two steps. It can be a passing emotion, like the warmth when a stranger returns your lost wallet, or a lasting trait. Most major traditions value it, from Stoicism and Buddhism to Christianity, Islam and Judaism.\n\nIn a 2003 study, Emmons and Michael McCullough asked students to write weekly for ten weeks about five things they were grateful for, five hassles, or five neutral events. The gratitude group felt more optimistic and positive about their lives, reported fewer physical complaints and exercised more than the hassles group. Later studies found similar, usually modest, benefits."],
    ["Thanking other people", "Thanking someone in person gives a lasting boost to both of you, and people underestimate how much it means.",
      "In a 2005 study led by Martin Seligman, participants wrote a letter to someone kind they'd never properly thanked, then read it to them in person. This 'gratitude visit' gave the largest short-term happiness boost of the exercises tested, lasting about a month.\n\nRecipients are usually more moved than writers expect. A 2018 study by Amit Kumar and Nicholas Epley found writers underestimated how surprised and happy recipients would feel, and overestimated how awkward it would be. Gratitude also binds people. Sara Algoe's 'find, remind and bind' theory says it helps us find good partners and friends and draws us closer to them. Adam Grant and Francesca Gino found that being thanked made people more willing to help others too, not just the person who thanked them."],
    ["Why it works", "Gratitude counters the brain's negativity bias and hedonic adaptation by redirecting attention to what is going well.",
      "The brain has a negativity bias: criticism, threats and losses grab attention more than praise, safety and gains. That helped our ancestors scan for danger, but it skews how we see an ordinary good day. Gratitude redirects attention to what is going well and balances the scales.\n\nIt also counters hedonic adaptation. Noticing a warm home, clean water or a reliable friend makes them feel fresh instead of invisible. And because gratitude focuses on what others have given, it turns attention outward, away from rumination about ourselves. Grateful people also tend to report better sleep, though that research is mostly correlational."],
    ["How to practise", "Be specific, focus on people, and keep the habit fresh rather than overdoing it.",
      "Quality beats quantity. In one study by Sonja Lyubomirsky and colleagues, people who counted their blessings once a week became happier, but those who did it three times a week didn't, perhaps because it turned into a chore. Be specific: not 'my family', but 'my sister calling to check on me when I was sick'.\n\nFocus on people more than possessions. Try imagining life without something good, like your health or a friendship. Link the habit to a routine, like the drive home or brushing your teeth. And say thanks out loud: a text to someone who helped you costs little and means more than you'd guess."],
    ["Honest about limits", "Gratitude exercises bring modest benefits, are no substitute for treatment, and shouldn't mean ignoring real problems.",
      "Reviews that pool dozens of studies find gratitude exercises give small but genuine gains in wellbeing. They often beat comparison activities only slightly, and effects on anxiety and depression are small. Many studies used university students and short follow-ups. Gratitude is free, quick and low risk, but it's one helpful habit among several, not a cure. Anyone with persistent low mood or anxiety should talk to a GP or mental health professional.\n\nIt isn't toxic positivity either. It doesn't mean ignoring injustice or being told to 'just be grateful' about unsafe conditions. It works best alongside acknowledging difficulties. You can be grieving or angry and still notice a kind neighbour. Both can be true."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the weekly gratitude-writing study find?", ["Writers became more anxious about the week ahead","Writers felt more optimistic about their lives than those who listed hassles","Writers felt no different from those who listed neutral events","Writers felt better but exercised less than the other groups"], 1, "Weekly counting of blessings improved optimism and wellbeing, though the benefits were usually modest."],
    [1, "How do people usually misjudge a letter of thanks?", ["They overestimate how surprised the recipient will be and underestimate the awkwardness","They assume the recipient will already know how grateful they are","They underestimate how happy the recipient will be and overestimate the awkwardness","They expect the recipient to respond with a thank-you of their own"], 2, "Recipients are more moved than writers expect, so thanks go unsent far more often than they should."],
    [2, "How does gratitude help counter the brain's negativity bias?", ["It redirects attention to what is going well, balancing what the brain overweights","It makes the brain forget past criticism and losses","It teaches us to ignore threats so we worry less","It replaces negative events with a list of imagined ones"], 0, "Gratitude deliberately shifts attention to good things that would otherwise go unnoticed."],
    [3, "Which gratitude habit is most likely to work?", ["Listing the same general things, like 'my family', every day","Writing about possessions you'd like to have","Specific entries about people, done often enough to stay fresh","Repeating it three times daily to build the habit quickly"], 2, "Specific, people-focused practice that doesn't become a chore keeps gratitude meaningful."],
    [4, "How does gratitude fit with real problems?", ["It replaces the need to deal with them","It works best alongside acknowledging difficulties","It is only helpful when nothing is wrong","It cures anxiety and depression when practised daily"], 1, "Gratitude is a modest, helpful habit, not a reason to ignore problems or skip professional help."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The thank-you most people never send may lift your mood for a month."});

WB.L("social-connection", "emotions", "beginner", "Friendship and loneliness",
  "Researchers compare the health risks of chronic loneliness to smoking. Friendship might be the most underrated health habit there is.",
  [
    ["Built to belong", "Belonging is a basic human need, and people with stronger relationships had about a 50% greater likelihood of survival in a large review.",
      "Humans evolved in groups, where being cut off meant danger: fewer people to share food, spot predators or care for you when sick. In 1995, Roy Baumeister and Mark Leary argued that the need to belong is a fundamental motivation, like hunger or thirst. Brain imaging suggests rejection activates some of the regions involved in physical pain, though the overlap is debated.\n\nThe health stakes are real. A 2010 review by Julianne Holt-Lunstad covered 148 studies and over 300,000 people. Those with stronger social relationships had about a 50% greater likelihood of survival over an average of about seven and a half years, an effect compared with risks like smoking and physical inactivity. Loneliness is also linked to heart disease, stroke, depression and dementia."],
    ["Alone versus lonely", "Loneliness is the gap between the connection you want and the connection you have, not simply being alone.",
      "Being alone isn't the same as being lonely. Loneliness is the painful feeling that your relationships fall short of your needs, in number or depth. Social isolation is an objective measure, like living alone. You can feel lonely in a crowded office, a share house or a marriage. Young adults report high loneliness, not just older people. In 2023, US Surgeon General Vivek Murthy issued an advisory on an 'epidemic of loneliness and isolation', and Australian surveys suggest a substantial share of adults often feel lonely.\n\nJohn Cacioppo argued loneliness works like a warning signal that we're socially unsafe. Lonely people slept less restfully, showed higher stress and inflammation, and read neutral faces as unfriendly, so they withdrew further. That wariness is a normal response to isolation, not proof that others dislike you."],
    ["How friendships form", "Friendships grow from many hours of repeated time together, and meaningful conversation builds closeness faster than small talk.",
      "Communication researcher Jeffrey Hall estimated in 2018 that it takes roughly 50 hours together to move from acquaintance to casual friend, about 90 hours to become a friend, and more than 200 hours to become close friends.\n\nWhat you do together matters too: joking around, catching up and meaningful conversation built closeness faster than small talk. Friendship grows from proximity and repetition, seeing the same people regularly at school, work, a club or a sports team. That's why adult friendships take more effort, and why regular shared activities matter so much. Time together is the raw ingredient."],
    ["Quality and small moments", "A few close relationships matter most for wellbeing, but even brief chats with strangers and acquaintances lift mood.",
      "Robin Dunbar suggested people can maintain roughly five very close relationships, about 15 good friends and 150 meaningful ones, the last figure known as Dunbar's number. The figures are rough and debated, but the inner circle matters most for wellbeing.\n\nLighter contact counts too. Chicago commuters asked by Nicholas Epley and Juliana Schroeder to chat with a stranger on the train enjoyed the ride more than those told to sit quietly, although most had predicted the opposite. Gillian Sandstrom and Elizabeth Dunn found that on days with more brief chats with 'weak ties', like baristas, classmates and neighbours, people felt happier and more connected."],
    ["Reaching out", "Reaching out is appreciated more than people expect, and technology helps when it leads to real contact.",
      "People underestimate how much others appreciate being contacted, especially out of the blue. So message the old friend, invite someone for a coffee or walk, and join something that meets regularly, like a community garden, sporting club, men's shed or choir. Volunteering is a good way in too. Show up consistently, since reliability builds trust.\n\nResearch on technology is mixed. Video calls and messages keep relationships alive across distance. Active use is linked to feeling more connected, while passive scrolling is linked to feeling worse, though not all research agrees. Ask whether an app leads to real contact or replaces it. If loneliness feels overwhelming or persistent, talk to a GP or counsellor. In Australia, Lifeline (13 11 14) is available 24 hours a day."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Holt-Lunstad's large review find about relationships and survival?", ["Close relationships made little difference to survival","Stronger relationships were linked to a roughly 50% greater likelihood of survival","Only family relationships were linked to longer life","Stronger relationships mattered less than physical activity"], 1, "Social connection matters for health on a scale comparable to well-known risks like smoking."],
    [1, "How does loneliness differ from being alone?", ["Loneliness only affects people who live by themselves","Loneliness is the gap between the connection you want and have","Loneliness is a personality trait found in introverts","Loneliness is a measure of how many friends you have"], 1, "People can feel lonely in a crowd and content alone; it is about unmet connection, not numbers."],
    [2, "What turns an acquaintance into a close friend?", ["A few intense shared experiences","Many hours of repeated time together, including meaningful conversation","Having plenty of interests in common","Messaging regularly online"], 1, "Close friendship takes well over 200 hours, and meaningful talk builds it faster than small talk."],
    [3, "What happened when Chicago commuters were asked to chat with a stranger?", ["They found it awkward and enjoyed the trip less","They enjoyed the ride more, though most had predicted the opposite","They enjoyed it only if the stranger started the chat","They felt no different from those who sat quietly"], 1, "We underestimate the pleasure of brief connections with strangers and acquaintances."],
    [4, "Which use of technology is most likely to help you feel connected?", ["Passively scrolling through other people's lives","Messaging friends in ways that lead to real contact","Replacing in-person meetings with group chats","Following lots of accounts that share your interests"], 1, "Active use that leads to real contact tends to help, while passive scrolling can leave you feeling worse."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Loneliness isn't about being alone. Find out what it is, and why it matters."});

WB.L("meaning", "emotions", "intermediate", "Meaning and purpose",
  "A happy life and a meaningful life aren't quite the same thing. Research suggests you need both.",
  [
    ["Happiness versus meaning", "Happiness is feeling good now, while meaning comes from purpose, contribution and a life that makes sense.",
      "A 2013 study by Roy Baumeister and colleagues surveyed about 400 Americans. Happiness was linked to getting what you want and feeling good in the present. Meaning was linked to reflecting on past and future, contributing to others and expressing who you are, and it sometimes came with stress and struggle. Takers were happier; givers found more meaning. Raising children can lower moment-to-moment happiness while bringing some of the deepest meaning people report.\n\nFrank Martela and Michael Steger proposed in 2016 that meaning has three parts: coherence (your life makes sense), purpose (goals and direction) and significance (your life matters to someone). A new retiree may keep coherence but lose the purpose work provided."],
    ["Frankl and purpose", "Viktor Frankl argued people can endure almost anything if they find meaning in it, and purpose is linked to better health.",
      "Austrian psychiatrist Viktor Frankl was deported by the Nazis in 1942 and survived several camps, including Auschwitz. His wife, parents and brother died in the Holocaust. In Man's Search for Meaning (1946), he argued that people can endure almost anything if they find meaning in it, and that we keep the freedom to choose our attitude. He saw meaning in creating, in love or beauty, and in how we face unavoidable suffering. His therapy is called logotherapy, from the Greek logos, 'meaning'.\n\nPurpose is also linked to lower risks of heart disease, stroke, dementia and early death, even after accounting for income and education. A 2019 analysis of about 7,000 Americans over 50 found the lowest purpose scores predicted earlier death. Cause is uncertain, as healthier people may find purpose easier."],
    ["Where meaning comes from", "Meaning usually comes from relationships, contribution, growth and something larger than yourself, found by doing rather than waiting.",
      "Research points to a few common sources of meaning. Close relationships come first for most people. Contributing matters too, through paid work, volunteering, parenting or caring for someone. So do growth and mastery, whether in carpentry, coaching or a language. Many find meaning in something larger: a community, cause, nature, faith or tradition. It's usually found by doing, and grows from commitments already made.\n\nIkigai, Japanese for roughly 'reason for being', often refers to everyday sources of purpose, like a hobby, a role in the family or community work, not a grand mission. The popular four-circle diagram is a Western addition. Aristotle's eudaimonia, flourishing through living well, is a similar idea."],
    ["Meaning at work", "The same job can feel like a job, a career or a calling, and job crafting can make work more meaningful.",
      "Psychologist Amy Wrzesniewski found people tend to see their work in one of three ways: as a job, done mainly for pay; a career, focused on advancement; or a calling, worthwhile in itself. Surprisingly, all three views appeared among people doing the same role, such as administrative assistants.\n\nIn a study of hospital cleaners, some saw the work as purely cleaning, while others emphasised comforting patients and helping nurses, and found it far more meaningful. Researchers call this job crafting: reshaping your tasks, relationships or how you think about your work to connect it with what you value."],
    ["Finding more meaning", "Small daily acts of contribution, living by your values and seeing hardship as part of growth build a meaningful life.",
      "You don't need a grand mission. At the end of each day, ask: what did I do that mattered to someone? Look for ways to use your strengths to help others, even in small ways. Reflect on your values and check whether your calendar matches them.\n\nTell your story, too. Psychologist Dan McAdams found that people who see their lives as stories of growth or redemption, where hard times led to something good, tend to report more meaning and wellbeing. Meaning can grow from difficulty, not just despite it, a point Frankl made from painful experience."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did Baumeister's study separate meaning from happiness?", ["Meaning was about feeling good now; happiness was about contributing to others","Happiness was about getting what you want now; meaning involved giving and reflecting on past and future","Happiness and meaning measured exactly the same thing","Meaning was linked to ease and comfort; happiness to struggle"], 1, "Meaning can come with stress and struggle, as raising children shows, while happiness is about feeling good in the moment."],
    [1, "What did Viktor Frankl argue about suffering?", ["People can endure almost anything if they find meaning in it","Suffering is only worthwhile when it brings pleasure later","Meaning disappears in extreme circumstances","Only those who choose their circumstances can find meaning"], 0, "Even in terrible circumstances, he argued, we keep the freedom to choose our attitude."],
    [2, "What does 'ikigai' usually refer to in everyday Japanese use?", ["A single grand life mission worth giving everything for","Ordinary sources of purpose, like a hobby, family role or community work","A balance of work and rest through the week","A diagram that matches your talents to a salary"], 1, "Ikigai means roughly 'reason for being', and it is usually found in everyday things, not a grand mission."],
    [3, "What is job crafting?", ["Choosing a career that matches your calling","Reshaping your tasks, relationships or thinking to connect work with your values","Negotiating a promotion to a more senior role","Taking on extra duties to get ahead"], 1, "Hospital cleaners who emphasised caring for patients found the same work far more meaningful."],
    [4, "Which daily habit helps build a meaningful life?", ["Waiting until you discover your one true purpose","Asking what you did that mattered to someone","Avoiding difficult experiences that cause stress","Focusing on activities that feel good in the moment"], 1, "Meaning grows from small acts of contribution and connection, not from a grand mission."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A happy life and a meaningful one aren't the same thing. What separates them?"});

WB.L("mindfulness", "emotions", "beginner", "Mindfulness and meditation",
  "Ancient practice, modern science. What mindfulness is, what the evidence says, and how to start in two minutes.",
  [
    ["What mindfulness is", "Mindfulness is paying attention to the present moment, on purpose and without judgement, which helps with a mind that wanders nearly half the time.",
      "Jon Kabat-Zinn founded the Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction program at the University of Massachusetts Medical School in 1979. He defines mindfulness as paying attention on purpose, in the present moment, non-judgementally. It has roots in Buddhist meditation, where the Pali word sati is often translated as mindfulness, but modern programs teach it as a secular skill. It's less about emptying the mind than noticing what's in it.\n\nMinds wander a lot. In a 2010 Harvard study, Matthew Killingsworth and Daniel Gilbert contacted about 2,250 adults through an iPhone app. Minds wandered about 47% of the time, and people were less happy when they did, even about pleasant topics. Mindfulness trains you to notice the drift and come back."],
    ["What the evidence says", "Mindfulness gives modest benefits for stress, anxiety and depression, but it isn't a cure-all and brain-change headlines deserve scepticism.",
      "Hundreds of trials suggest mindfulness programs give small-to-moderate benefits for stress, anxiety and depression, roughly comparable to other active approaches like exercise or relaxation training. Mindfulness-based cognitive therapy, an eight-week group program, reduces relapse in people with repeated depression and is recommended for that in some national guidelines. Early research was often small or poorly controlled. It doesn't suit everyone: for people with trauma histories, intensive meditation can bring up distressing experiences, so a trained teacher matters.\n\nBe sceptical of brain claims too. Small early studies reported grey matter changes after eight weeks, but a 2022 University of Wisconsin analysis of two randomised trials found no clear structural changes. Judge mindfulness by whether it helps you."],
    ["A simple practice", "Focus on the breath, notice when your mind wanders, and gently return.",
      "Sit comfortably, with your back upright but not stiff. Close your eyes or lower your gaze. Notice the physical feeling of breathing, perhaps the cool air at the nostrils or the rise and fall of your belly.\n\nBefore long your mind will wander to plans, memories or an itch. That's normal, not a failure. The moment you notice, gently bring attention back to the breath, without scolding yourself. That noticing and returning is the exercise, like a repetition in the gym. Start with two to five minutes a day at a regular time, and build up slowly if you enjoy it."],
    ["Other styles of practice", "Body scans, walking meditation and loving-kindness practice offer alternatives to breath focus.",
      "Breath focus isn't the only option. A body scan moves attention slowly from the toes to the head, noticing sensations without trying to change them, and it's a core part of MBSR. Walking meditation attends to each step and the shifting weight of the body, which suits people who find sitting still hard.\n\nLoving-kindness meditation, drawn from the Buddhist practice of metta, involves silently wishing wellbeing to yourself, loved ones, strangers and even difficult people. Some studies link it to increased positive emotion. Guided recordings, including free ones from Australian not-for-profit Smiling Mind, can help beginners get started."],
    ["Attention in daily life", "You can practise mindfulness during everyday activities, much as the Stoics practised continuous attention to their own thoughts.",
      "The Stoics didn't use the word mindfulness, but they practised prosoche, continuous attention to one's own thoughts and judgements. Epictetus urged students to examine each impression before accepting it. Marcus Aurelius, Roman emperor from 161 to 180 CE, reminded himself in his Meditations to focus on the task in front of him and not be carried away by imagined future troubles.\n\nYou don't need formal meditation to practise. Eat the first few bites of a meal slowly. Notice your feet and the sounds around you on the walk to the bus. Take three conscious breaths before answering a stressful email. Pause before picking up your phone and ask whether you want to. These moments break autopilot habits and create a gap between what happens and how you react."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does mindfulness actually involve?", ["Emptying the mind of all thoughts","Noticing the present moment and what is in your mind, without judgement","Thinking deeply about plans and problems","Relaxing the body until you feel sleepy"], 1, "Mindfulness is about noticing what's in your mind, including its tendency to wander, not about emptying it."],
    [1, "What does the evidence say about mindfulness programs?", ["They cure anxiety and depression for most people","They give modest benefits, roughly comparable to other active approaches","They have no effect beyond a placebo","They clearly outperform exercise and relaxation training"], 1, "Mindfulness is a helpful tool with small-to-moderate benefits, not a miracle cure."],
    [2, "What should you do when your mind wanders during meditation?", ["Restart the session to get a clean attempt","Push the thought away firmly","Gently return attention to the breath, since that is the exercise","Follow the thought through, then return"], 2, "Noticing the drift and returning is the repetition that builds attention, so wandering isn't failure."],
    [3, "Which practice suits someone who finds sitting still hard?", ["A longer breath-focus session","Walking meditation, attending to each step","A body scan lying completely still","Guided recordings played at bedtime"], 1, "Walking meditation uses the same attention skills while moving."],
    [4, "What did the Stoic practice of prosoche have in common with mindfulness?", ["Continuous attention to one's own thoughts and judgements","Fasting to build self-discipline","Avoiding all strong emotions","Sitting in silence for long periods"], 0, "Both traditions suggest that calmly watching our reactions gives us more freedom in how we respond."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your mind wanders nearly half the time. What it costs, and what to do."});

WB.L("attachment", "emotions", "intermediate", "Attachment styles",
  "The bonds you formed as a baby may echo in how you love as an adult, though less rigidly than the internet suggests.",
  [
    ["Bowlby's big idea", "Infants have an evolved need to stay close to caregivers for safety, and a responsive caregiver becomes a secure base.",
      "In the 1950s, British psychiatrist John Bowlby challenged the popular idea that babies attach to their mothers mainly because they provide food. Drawing on work with children separated from their families, including children in hospitals and institutions, he argued that humans have an evolved attachment system. Infants seek closeness to caregivers, especially when frightened, tired or sick, because staying near protectors helped survival.\n\nA caregiver who responds reliably becomes a 'secure base' from which the child can explore the world. Harry Harlow's ethically troubling experiments, in which infant monkeys clung to soft cloth 'mothers' rather than wire ones holding milk, supported similar ideas."],
    ["The Strange Situation", "Toddlers' reactions to separation and reunion reveal secure, anxious, avoidant or disorganised attachment, shaped by caregiver sensitivity and other factors.",
      "Mary Ainsworth, who worked with Bowlby, designed the Strange Situation in the 1970s. A toddler of about one plays in an unfamiliar room, the parent leaves briefly, a stranger enters, and the parent returns. Most children were upset but quickly comforted on reunion, then went back to play: securely attached. Others were very distressed and hard to soothe, sometimes angry (anxious or ambivalent), or seemed indifferent and avoided the parent (avoidant). Mary Main and Judith Solomon later identified a disorganised pattern, with children confused or frightened.\n\nSensitive, responsive caregiving makes secure attachment more likely, but the link is moderate: temperament, stress, poverty and health matter too. Secure attachment is the most common pattern across countries, about 60% in many samples, and babies also bond with fathers, grandparents and other carers."],
    ["Attachment in adult love", "Adults show secure, anxious or avoidant patterns, and anxious and avoidant partners can lock into a chase-and-withdraw cycle.",
      "In 1987, Cindy Hazan and Phillip Shaver applied attachment ideas to adult love, using a 'love quiz' printed in a newspaper. Secure adults find it fairly easy to get close, depend on partners and be depended on. Anxious adults crave closeness but fear abandonment, seek reassurance and may read a slow reply as a bad sign. Avoidant adults feel uncomfortable with closeness, value independence and may shut down in emotional conversations. Researchers now measure two scales, anxiety and avoidance, so most people are a mix.\n\nThe two can lock into a pursue-withdraw cycle: the anxious partner chases closeness, the avoidant partner feels crowded and pulls away, and the chasing intensifies. Seeing it as a pattern you create together, and stating needs clearly ('I feel reassured when you text if you're running late'), helps break it."],
    ["Repair and change", "Secure attachment grows from reliable care and repair, not perfection, and styles can shift through 'earned security'.",
      "Secure attachment doesn't require perfect parents. Donald Winnicott coined the phrase 'good enough mother' in the 1950s for care that meets a child's needs reasonably well. Edward Tronick found that even healthy parent-baby pairs are often out of sync; what matters is how reliably mismatches get repaired. In his 'still face' experiment, babies grew distressed when a parent's face went blank, then recovered when warm contact returned. Adult relationships work the same way: ruptures are normal, and repair builds trust.\n\nAttachment is moderately stable but not destiny. Many people develop 'earned security' through a supportive relationship, good therapy or reflecting on early experiences. Research with the Adult Attachment Interview suggests how coherently people tell their childhood story matters more than whether it was easy."],
    ["Using it wisely", "Attachment ideas are useful for self-understanding, not for labelling exes or diagnosing partners.",
      "Attachment theory is well supported by decades of research, but online it's often oversimplified into rigid types used to label exes or diagnose dates after a few messages. It isn't a clinical diagnosis, and a single quiz can't capture something that varies across relationships and over time. Life events can move people either way: a loving partner may help an anxious person relax, while a painful breakup can push someone towards insecurity.\n\nIt's most useful as a mirror: noticing your own patterns, like reassurance-seeking or pulling away when things get close, and choosing how to respond. The qualities linked to secure relationships can be practised by anyone: responsiveness, reliability, honesty about needs, and repair after conflict. If old patterns feel stuck, a psychologist or couples counsellor can help."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do infants seek closeness to caregivers, according to Bowlby?", ["Because caregivers provide food","Because staying near protectors helped survival, through an evolved attachment system","Because they learn it by copying adults","Because early separation is always harmful"], 1, "A reliable caregiver acts as a secure base, letting the child explore the world."],
    [1, "How does a securely attached toddler behave in the Strange Situation?", ["Shows no distress when the parent leaves","Is upset by separation but quickly comforted on reunion, then returns to play","Is unable to be soothed when the parent returns","Avoids the parent on return"], 1, "Secure children use the parent as a base and recover quickly after being upset."],
    [2, "What is the anxious-avoidant trap?", ["Both partners avoid talking about their needs","The anxious partner chases closeness, the avoidant partner withdraws, and each intensifies the other","The avoidant partner takes on the caring role","A pattern that only appears in new relationships"], 1, "Seeing the cycle as shared, not one person's fault, helps both partners step out of it."],
    [3, "What matters most in parent and baby interactions, according to Tronick?", ["Avoiding any mismatch or frustration","How reliably mismatches get repaired","Following a strict routine","Giving constant attention"], 1, "Ruptures are normal; repairing them reliably builds trust, in childhood and in adult relationships."],
    [4, "How is attachment theory best used?", ["To diagnose a partner after a few dates","To sort people into fixed types that never change","To notice your own patterns and choose how to respond","To predict whether a relationship will last"], 2, "Attachment ideas help self-understanding, but they are not a clinical diagnosis or a label for others."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A baby's reaction to a parent leaving the room says a surprising amount."});

WB.L("love-science", "emotions", "beginner", "The science of love",
  "Chemistry, psychology and a famous 36 questions. What researchers have learned about falling, and staying, in love.",
  [
    ["Love on the brain", "Early romantic love activates the brain's dopamine reward system, and Fisher proposed three overlapping systems: lust, attraction and attachment.",
      "In a 2005 study, Helen Fisher, Arthur Aron and colleagues scanned people newly and intensely in love as they viewed photos of their beloved. Dopamine-rich regions, including the ventral tegmental area and parts of the caudate nucleus, became more active: the same reward circuitry that responds to food, winning money and some drugs. That helps explain the obsessive thinking, energy and craving of early love, and why rejection can feel like withdrawal. Scans of recently rejected lovers also showed activity in reward and craving regions.\n\nFisher proposed three systems: lust, driven by hormones like testosterone and oestrogen; attraction, linked to dopamine and focused on one person; and attachment, linked to oxytocin and vasopressin, which creates the calm bond that helps partners stay together. They can occur separately. It's a useful model, not settled fact."],
    ["Who we fall for", "Proximity, familiarity, similarity and being liked back predict attraction, and gradual mutual self-disclosure builds closeness.",
      "We tend to fall for people we see often: classmates, coworkers, neighbours, teammates. A 1950 study of MIT student housing by Leon Festinger and colleagues found friendships formed most often between people living closest together. Repeated exposure increases liking, the mere-exposure effect. We favour people similar in values, background and interests; 'opposites attract' gets little research support. We also like people who like us back. Looks matter most at first meetings and less as people get to know each other.\n\nCloseness can be built, too. In 1997, Arthur Aron's team had pairs of strangers spend 45 minutes taking turns through 36 increasingly personal questions, from light ones to memories, regrets and problems. Pairs felt much closer than those making small talk. The aim was closeness, not romance, and it works for friendships too."],
    ["Culture, passion and time", "Romantic love appears in nearly all cultures studied, and intense passion usually fades while companionate love can keep growing.",
      "Some claim romantic love was invented by medieval European poets. The evidence disagrees. In 1992, William Jankowiak and Edward Fischer searched ethnographic records from 166 cultures and found clear evidence of romantic love in about 89%, with most of the rest lacking enough information. Love poems survive from ancient Egypt. What differs is how much weight love gets when choosing a partner: in some societies families lead matchmaking and love is expected to grow after marriage.\n\nPassionate love, the intense early longing, usually declines over the first few years. Companionate love, a deep friendship of trust, affection and shared life, can keep growing, and many long-married couples report high satisfaction. Robert Sternberg's 1986 triangular theory mixes intimacy, passion and commitment; consummate love combines all three."],
    ["Novelty and excitement", "Doing new and exciting things together can boost relationship satisfaction.",
      "Arthur Aron's self-expansion theory proposes that we're drawn to partners who broaden our lives, and that relationships feel most alive when they keep adding new experiences. In a 2000 study, couples who crossed a gym-mat obstacle course while strapped together at the wrist and ankle reported greater relationship satisfaction afterwards than couples given a mundane task.\n\nA famous 1974 experiment by Donald Dutton and Aron, on a swaying suspension bridge in Canada, suggested people can partly mistake the arousal of fear or excitement for attraction. The practical lesson: keep trying new things together, from a hike to a cooking class."],
    ["What keeps couples together", "Responding to each other's everyday bids for attention, plus kindness and avoiding contempt, predict lasting relationships.",
      "Psychologist John Gottman observed thousands of couples in his University of Washington 'love lab'. Stable couples responded to each other's 'bids' for attention, like a comment about a bird outside or a hand on the shoulder, far more often than couples who later split. In one study of newlyweds, those still together six years later had turned towards bids about 86% of the time, versus about 33% for those who divorced.\n\nStable couples also kept around five positive interactions for every negative one during conflict. Gottman identified contempt, such as eye-rolling and mockery, as the strongest predictor of breakups. Everyday kindness matters more than grand gestures."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does early romantic love activate in the brain?", ["The pain system, like a physical injury","The dopamine reward system, like other powerful rewards","The stress system, like a threat","The memory system, like a familiar place"], 1, "Early love uses the same reward circuitry as food and winning money, which explains its craving and focus."],
    [1, "What made the 36 questions build closeness between strangers?", ["Gradual, mutual self-disclosure","Light humour throughout","Trying to impress each other","Quick answers and fast pace"], 0, "Sharing increasingly personal things in turn creates closeness, whether in romance or friendship."],
    [2, "How does love typically change over the years?", ["Passion stays at the same pitch, while companionate love fades","Passion usually fades over a few years, while companionate love can keep deepening","Both passion and companionate love usually vanish by middle age","Companionate love is a weaker form of romantic love that cannot grow"], 1, "Fading passion is normal, and a deep friendship of trust and affection can keep growing."],
    [3, "What did the couples' obstacle-course study suggest?", ["Familiar routines are the best way to keep satisfaction high","Doing novel, mildly challenging things together boosts relationship satisfaction","Competing against each other improves a relationship","Couples should avoid anything that causes stress"], 1, "New shared experiences keep a relationship feeling alive."],
    [4, "Which habit best distinguished couples who stayed together?", ["Turning towards each other's everyday bids for attention","Avoiding any disagreement","Planning grand romantic gestures","Spending most free time together"], 0, "Small, everyday acts of attention and kindness matter more than grand gestures."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Opposites rarely attract. What actually predicts who we fall for?"});

WB.L("conflict", "emotions", "intermediate", "Healthy conflict",
  "Couples, friends and colleagues who never argue aren't necessarily healthy. It's how you fight that matters.",
  [
    ["Conflict is normal", "Most couple conflicts are perpetual problems that never fully go away, so the goal is handling them well.",
      "Disagreement is inevitable when people share homes or workplaces. John Gottman found about 69% of couples' conflicts are 'perpetual problems', rooted in lasting differences in personality, values or lifestyle, like a tidy partner and a relaxed one, or differing views on spending. They never fully go away. Happy couples learn to discuss them with humour, affection and respect. Avoiding all conflict lets resentment build quietly.\n\nHow you handle conflict matters. Kenneth Thomas and Ralph Kilmann described five styles: competing, accommodating, avoiding, compromising and collaborating. Most people lean towards one or two by habit, often learned in their family. None is always right: avoiding suits trivial issues, competing may suit emergencies, and collaborating suits important problems where the relationship matters."],
    ["Start soft", "How a conversation begins strongly predicts how it ends, so raise specific complaints gently rather than criticising character.",
      "Gottman found the first three minutes of a couple's conflict discussion predicted how it would end, and even predicted divorce years later with surprising accuracy. A 'harsh start-up' begins with criticism, blame or sarcasm and usually ends badly. A 'soft start-up' describes the situation without blame, uses 'I' statements and states a positive need: 'I'm stressed about the dishes piling up. Could we work out a system?' rather than 'You never do anything around here.' Avoid raising problems when you're tired, hungry or rushing out the door.\n\nComplain about specific behaviour, not character. 'You didn't call when you were late, and I was worried' is a complaint. 'You're so selfish' is criticism, which provokes defensiveness. 'Always' and 'never' invite arguments about exceptions. Tackle one issue at a time."],
    ["Listen and accept influence", "Understanding the other person's view, and taking their requests seriously, calms conflict even without agreement.",
      "In conflict, we usually listen just long enough to prepare our defence. Instead, summarise the other person's point first: 'So you feel I don't take your job seriously?' Let them correct you until they agree you've got it. Feeling understood lowers the temperature. Look for the need behind positions: a money argument may really be about security, freedom or respect. Ask curious questions rather than cross-examining. Understanding isn't agreeing, but it often opens a path to compromise.\n\nGottman also highlighted accepting influence: taking your partner's opinions and requests seriously, finding the part you can agree with and being willing to change your plans. In his newlywed study, marriages were happier and more stable when husbands accepted influence from their wives. Sharing power isn't losing."],
    ["Pause, then repair", "Pause when flooded with emotion, then make repair attempts, because repair predicts success better than avoiding mistakes.",
      "When heart rate rises sharply and emotions flood, clear reasoning becomes difficult. Gottman called this 'flooding' and linked it to heart rates above roughly 100 beats per minute. Agree on a signal to pause, take at least 20 minutes to calm down doing something soothing rather than rehearsing arguments, then return to the conversation at an agreed time.\n\nAfter conflict, repair matters: a genuine apology, a joke, a touch, or simply 'I see your point'. Gottman found the best predictor of success wasn't avoiding mistakes but making effective repair attempts, and partners accepting them. Couples who can repair can survive plenty of arguments."],
    ["Fighting fair at work", "At work, raise issues early, describe behaviour and impact, and focus on problems and interests rather than people and positions.",
      "The same principles work at work. Raise issues early and privately, before they harden into grudges. Describe behaviour and its impact, not character: 'The report arrived after the deadline, so I couldn't review it' rather than 'You're unreliable.' Ask questions to understand the other side's pressures and constraints.\n\nThe negotiation classic Getting to Yes, by Roger Fisher and William Ury of the Harvard Negotiation Project, advises separating the people from the problem and focusing on interests rather than positions. Research on teams suggests open disagreement about ideas can improve decisions when trust is high, while personal conflict reliably hurts."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does Gottman's research say about most couple conflicts?", ["Most are caused by poor communication and can be fully solved","Most are perpetual problems that never fully go away, so handling them well matters","Most are about money and can be solved with a budget","Most disappear after the first few years"], 1, "Happy couples don't solve every disagreement; they discuss lasting differences with humour, affection and respect."],
    [1, "Which is a soft start-up?", ["You never do anything around here","I'm stressed about the dishes piling up. Could we work out a system?","We need to talk about your attitude to housework","If you cared, you'd have done the dishes by now"], 1, "A soft start-up describes the situation without blame and states a positive need."],
    [2, "Why summarise the other person's point before replying?", ["It gives you time to plan your argument","Feeling understood lowers the temperature and can open a path to compromise","It shows you agree with their position","It lets you correct anything they got wrong"], 1, "Understanding isn't agreeing, but it often reveals compromises neither person could see before."],
    [3, "What best predicted success after an argument, according to Gottman?", ["Never making mistakes","Making repair attempts, and partners accepting them","Winning the argument decisively","Waiting several days before talking again"], 1, "Couples who can repair can survive plenty of arguments."],
    [4, "When can open disagreement about ideas improve team decisions?", ["When it is kept quiet and private","When trust is high","When it becomes personal","When a manager makes the final call"], 1, "Disagreement about ideas can help when trust is high; personal conflict reliably hurts."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The first three minutes of an argument can predict how it ends."});

WB.L("boundaries", "emotions", "beginner", "Setting boundaries",
  "Boundaries aren't walls or ultimatums. They're clear, kind statements about what you will and won't do.",
  [
    ["What boundaries are", "A boundary is a limit on your own actions that protects what you'll accept, not a demand on someone else.",
      "A boundary is a limit you set for yourself: what you'll accept, how much time or energy you'll give, and what you'll share. Healthy boundaries let you stay close to people without losing yourself. They come in several kinds. Physical boundaries cover personal space and touch. Emotional boundaries mean not taking responsibility for other people's feelings. Time boundaries might mean not answering work emails at night. Material boundaries cover lending money or belongings. They're like fences with gates you choose when to open.\n\nA common mistake is trying to control others. 'You can't talk to me like that' is really a demand. A boundary focuses on what you'll do: 'If you keep shouting, I'll leave the room and we can talk later.' You can't make other people change, but you can decide how you respond."],
    ["Why they're hard", "Guilt, fear of conflict and wanting to be liked make boundaries hard, but resentment, dread and exhaustion signal one is needed.",
      "People-pleasers often say yes when they mean no, then feel resentful, exhausted or quietly taken for granted. Setting a boundary can trigger guilt, fear of conflict or fear of rejection. Some people grew up in families where their needs weren't respected, so limits feel selfish or risky. Some workplaces reward always being available. Without boundaries, people often burn out or quietly withdraw. A clear boundary is often kinder than a reluctant yes.\n\nMany people don't know their limits until they're crossed, but feelings are clues. Resentment signals you're giving more than you want to. Dread before seeing someone, or relief when plans are cancelled, may point to something needing attention. Exhaustion can mean you're overextended. Ask what is bothering you: the amount of time, the topic, or the way you're spoken to?"],
    ["Saying it and holding it", "State boundaries clearly, briefly and kindly, then follow through consistently.",
      "Effective boundaries are simple: 'I'm not able to take that on this week.' 'I need to leave by nine.' Long justifications invite debate. You can be warm and firm: 'I love seeing you, and I need you to call before coming over.' Expect pushback, especially from people who benefited from your lack of boundaries. Calm repetition works better than escalating. You can also take time: 'Let me think about it and get back to you.'\n\nA boundary only works with follow-through. If you say you'll leave when someone shouts and then stay, people learn your limits are negotiable. Follow-through needs no anger, just consistency. Start with low-stakes boundaries, like not replying to non-urgent messages late at night. Expect some discomfort, including guilt; it doesn't mean you've done something wrong. Over time, people generally adjust."],
    ["Boundaries at work", "Australian law gives many employees a right to disconnect from work contact outside working hours, unless refusing is unreasonable.",
      "Work is one of the hardest places to set limits, especially when phones make everyone reachable at all hours. In Australia, a 'right to disconnect' was added to the Fair Work Act, applying to most employers from August 2024 and to small businesses from August 2025.\n\nIt lets employees refuse to monitor, read or respond to work contact outside their working hours unless the refusal is unreasonable, taking into account things like their role, pay and the reason for the contact. Beyond the law, practical steps help: agree on response times with your team, turn off notifications after hours, and negotiate priorities rather than silently absorbing extra work."],
    ["Respecting others' boundaries", "Healthy relationships respect boundaries in both directions, and ignoring or punishing boundaries is a warning sign.",
      "Boundaries go both ways. When someone says no, accept it without pushing, sulking, guilt-tripping or punishing them later. Notice if you react badly to other people's limits, and ask why: is it hurt, fear, or a sense of entitlement? Respecting boundaries builds trust, because people feel safe being honest with you.\n\nA relationship where one person's boundaries are consistently ignored, mocked or punished is a warning sign that may involve control or abuse. In Australia, confidential support is available through 1800RESPECT (1800 737 732), and anyone in immediate danger should call 000."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which is a well-phrased boundary?", ["You need to stop talking to me like that","If you keep shouting, I'll leave the room and we can talk later","Please try to speak to me more respectfully","I won't tolerate you being rude to me"], 1, "A boundary focuses on your own actions, which you can control, rather than demanding that someone else change."],
    [1, "What do resentment, dread and exhaustion often signal?", ["That the other person is behaving badly on purpose","That a boundary may be needed","That the relationship should end","That you are being too sensitive"], 1, "These feelings are useful clues that you're giving more than you want to or are overextended."],
    [2, "Why does follow-through matter for a boundary?", ["It shows the other person who is in charge","People learn your limits are negotiable if you don't act on them","It makes the other person feel guilty enough to change","It removes any discomfort straight away"], 1, "Calm consistency teaches people what to expect, without needing anger or a lecture."],
    [3, "What does Australia's right to disconnect allow employees to do?", ["Refuse any work contact outside hours in every circumstance","Refuse work contact outside working hours unless refusing is unreasonable","Refuse extra duties during working hours","Choose their own working hours"], 1, "Reasonableness depends on factors like the role, pay and the reason for the contact."],
    [4, "What is a warning sign in a relationship?", ["Both people sometimes saying no to each other","One person's boundaries are consistently ignored, mocked or punished","One partner asking for time to think before agreeing","Needing to repeat a boundary calmly"], 1, "Healthy relationships respect boundaries both ways, and persistent disrespect may involve control or abuse."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why a firm 'no' can be the kinder answer, and how to say it well."});

WB.L("adult-friendship", "emotions", "beginner", "Making friends as an adult",
  "School gave us friends by default. As adults, we have to make them on purpose. Here's what works.",
  [
    ["Why it gets harder", "Friendship grows from proximity, repeated unplanned contact and openness, which school provided and adult life scatters.",
      "At school and university, friendships formed almost automatically: you saw the same people daily and had free time. Adult life scatters people through work, moves, relationships and children. Many people make fewer new friends from their late twenties. Surveys suggest many adults have fewer close friends than they'd like, and men in particular often report fewer people to confide in. Friendship needs a more intentional approach, like fitness or a career.\n\nSociologists, including Rebecca Adams, describe three conditions for close friendship: proximity, repeated and unplanned interactions, and a setting that encourages people to confide. School, team sports and share houses provide all three. As an adult, you can recreate them by joining things that meet regularly, like a club, class, choir, volunteer group or sports team. A single event doesn't give you repeated, casual encounters."],
    ["Show up and initiate", "Regular attendance builds trust, and suggesting plans yourself helps, because most people wait to be invited.",
      "Going to one event rarely creates a friend. Going to the same weekly activity for months often does. Familiarity builds trust, partly through the mere-exposure effect: we tend to like what's familiar. Close friendships take many hours, so at a two-hour weekly activity, expect months, not weeks. Pick something you'd enjoy anyway, and stay afterwards for a chat.\n\nMany potential friendships stall because both people wait for the other to suggest something. Be the initiator, and be specific about time and place, since 'we should catch up sometime' rarely happens. Suggest something low-pressure, like a walk or coffee. Don't read one declined invitation as rejection, since people's lives are full. Try again, or suggest a different time."],
    ["Assume people like you", "People who expect to be accepted act warmer, and we consistently underestimate how much new acquaintances like us.",
      "Research on the 'acceptance prophecy', led by psychologist Danu Anthony Stinson, found that people who expect to be accepted act warmer and more open, which leads others to actually accept them. Expecting rejection often makes people guarded, quiet or cold, which can create the rejection they fear.\n\nStudies of the 'liking gap' by Erica Boothby and colleagues found that after a conversation, people consistently underestimated how much their new acquaintance liked them and enjoyed their company. So assume the best and act accordingly: smile, ask questions and show interest. Most people are privately worrying about the same things you are."],
    ["Old ties and change", "Reconnecting with dormant friends is easier than expected, and friendships naturally drift and change.",
      "Some of the easiest friendships to grow are ones you already have. In a 2011 study, Daniel Levin, Jorge Walter and Keith Murnighan asked executives to reconnect with contacts they hadn't spoken to for at least three years. These ties often gave more valuable and novel advice than current ones, and people had worried more than necessary about awkwardness. A simple message, like 'I was thinking of you the other day, how have you been?', is often enough.\n\nNot every friendship lasts, and that isn't always failure. A Dutch study by Gerald Mollenhorst found that over seven years, people kept only about half of their close network, with new people replacing those who drifted. Friendships fade when circumstances change, and some can be revived. Holding friendships a little loosely, with gratitude, makes room for new ones."],
    ["Deepening friendships", "Friendships deepen through gradual self-disclosure, support in hard times, shared experiences and enthusiastic responses to good news.",
      "Moving from casual to close friendship involves sharing more personal things gradually, being there in hard times, and creating shared memories, like a weekend away or a project together. Remember details and follow up: 'How did the job interview go?'\n\nCelebrate their good news genuinely. Psychologist Shelly Gable found that responding to good news with enthusiasm and interest, which she called active-constructive responding, is linked to closer, more satisfying relationships, and may matter as much as support in bad times. Keep contact going even when life is busy: a quick message, a shared joke or a standing monthly catch-up keeps a friendship alive between longer visits."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which setting best matches the conditions for close friendship?", ["A one-off party with many new people","A weekly club or team, where you repeatedly meet the same people","A large online forum","A long conference with a social event"], 1, "Proximity, repeated casual contact and openness are what clubs and teams provide."],
    [1, "Why do many potential friendships stall?", ["Most adults aren't interested in new friends","Both people wait for the other to suggest plans","People mostly prefer to meet in groups","There are too few places to meet"], 1, "Being specific and taking the initiative makes a big difference."],
    [2, "What is the 'acceptance prophecy'?", ["People who expect acceptance act warmer, which leads to acceptance","People who are accepted at first lose interest later","Friendships work best when you hide your faults","People like those who seem hard to get"], 0, "Expecting rejection makes people guarded, which can create the very rejection they fear."],
    [3, "What did research find about reconnecting with dormant contacts?", ["It rarely works and usually feels awkward","Reconnected ties often gave more valuable and novel advice than current ones","It works only if you share news first","It is best saved for professional contacts"], 1, "People worry more about awkwardness than they need to, so a simple message is often enough."],
    [4, "What helps friendships deepen?", ["Keeping conversations light and avoiding hard topics","Gradual self-disclosure, support and shared experiences","Seeing each other only on special occasions","Matching each other's achievements"], 1, "Responding with enthusiasm to good news also builds closer, more satisfying friendships."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Close friends take over 200 hours to make. Where does the time come from?"});

